From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Wed, 1 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: kendryte-hash: rework the sha256 data path

The driver never ran correctly, and nothing called it:

 - dma_write_rwcfg() freed the coherent input buffer before the DMA
   started, so the engine read freed memory.
 - init() ioremapped the PUF DMA and crypto blocks into file-scope
   pointers and final() unmapped them, with no lock, so two requests in
   flight used each other's mappings, and the AES driver programs the
   same PUF DMA channel.
 - final() returned 0 when the engine failed, export() and import() did
   nothing, the busy wait had no timeout, and the kzalloc() result was
   not checked.

Rewrite the data path:

 - One 16 KiB coherent bounce buffer, allocated at probe. Each DMA block
   is copied in; it is reused only after the engine is idle. A streaming
   buffer synced for the device does not work here: about one block in
   40000, typically a short continuation block written over the previous
   one, is hashed from stale bytes, with no DMA or hash status error,
   and the same block run again comes out right.
 - Every programming sequence holds the kendryte-puf lock. Register
   blocks are mapped once at probe.
 - The request context holds the engine state, the length counters and
   the unsent tail, so export() and import() copy it whole.
 - Errors and a 100 ms DMA timeout are returned to the caller.
 - finup() and digest() send a message that fits the bounce buffer as a
   single head-and-tail block. The empty message returns the known
   digest without the engine.

The register sequence is unchanged. The engine is driven under a mutex,
so the priority drops from 300 to 50, below sha256-generic: kernel users
and AF_ALG "sha256" keep the generic code, and a caller selects the
engine by its driver name, "sha256-kendryte".

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
